[ ] 28 Host-managed shared profile sessions (plugin ABI v6). Today a profile
       (ABI v5, item 27) is a CONFIG store: a node resolves a profile-ref at
       fire time, gets host/port/credentials, and opens its own connection. That
       is fine for one-shot HTTP-style work but breaks down whenever the
       underlying resource is single-owner or stateful: a USB-serial device can
       only be held by one fd, and an MQTT broker session is logically one TCP
       socket per (broker,client-id) that wants its subscriptions and outbound
       publishes funnelled through one place. The Meshtastic node already paid
       this tax (one device <-> one node, no multi-node fan-out possible) and
       the upcoming pipnode-zigbee-plugin would inherit the same limitation (the
       GW70-MQTT CC2652 dongle speaks ZNP over /dev/ttyUSB0; multiple debug
       nodes -- status, list-devices, dump-frames, permit-join -- all need to
       share that one fd, queue requests serially, and fan unsolicited frames
       out to subscribers).

       Fix it at the host. Keep the existing profile as the CONFIG layer, and
       add a LIVE-RESOURCE layer on top: PnProfileSession, a per-profile-id
       object opened on first reference and torn down on last release, owned and
       ref-counted by the host. The plugin teaches the host how to build one via
       two new vfuncs on PnProfileSchemaClass:
           PnProfileSession *(*open_session)  (PnProfile *cfg, GError **error);
           void              (*close_session) (PnProfileSession *session);
       Host-side API the nodes use:
           PnProfileSession *pn_profile_acquire_session (const gchar *profile_id,
                                                         GError **);
           void              pn_profile_release_session (PnProfileSession *);
       Acquire in constructed(), release in dispose(), exactly mirroring the
       existing pn_node_get_profile lifecycle. Re-emit PnVault::changed as a
       session teardown+rebuild so a credential edit propagates the same way it
       does today. State changes (device unplug, broker disconnect) fan out via
       a PnProfileSession::state-changed signal mirroring PnVault::changed.
       Concurrency model: single-threaded GLib mainloop, GIOChannel/GUnixIO on
       the fd, all callbacks on the main thread (no new threads -- pipnode does
       not use them elsewhere). Lives in libpipnode-core so pipnode-run gets the
       same shared sessions headlessly (see item 23).

       The session object itself is plugin-defined. For Zigbee that means a ZNP
       transport with a FIFO request queue (pn_znp_request(session, cmd,
       payload, callback)) plus a subscriber registry for unsolicited frames
       (pn_znp_subscribe(session, type, callback)); for MQTT it is a libmosq
       client with one TCP connection, a subscribe-multiplex table and a publish
       queue; for Meshtastic a serial-mesh session of the same shape as Zigbee.

       Pilot/port order, smallest blast radius first: (a) ship the host-side
       registry + the two vfuncs + the ABI bump 5 -> 6, no plugin work; (b) port
       the bundled MQTT Source/Sink in plugins/network onto it -- the win is
       immediate and observable (N MQTT nodes against one broker stop dialling N
       sockets, and their subscriptions merge into one), and it proves the API
       on existing test coverage; (c) build the Zigbee plugin greenfield against
       the now-proven API (GW70Profile + ZnpSession + small debug nodes -- see
       item 29 if/when filed); (d) retrofit the Meshtastic node to lift its
       one-device-one-node ceiling; (e) opportunistically: the kodi plugin's
       JSON-RPC client (multiple kodi nodes currently each open their own
       libsoup session), shell SSH, any future stateful-socket plugin.

       Trade-offs to nail in design: ownership boundary (plugin defines what a
       session IS, host owns the registry + ref-count + lifetime; plugin never
       caches a PnProfileSession* outside an acquire/release pair), error
       propagation (every subscribed node must learn when the device disappears
       -- state-changed signal), and the headless story (sessions must work
       under pipnode-run, which is why the layer lives in core not gui). Same
       ABI-bump drill as item 27: sudo make install + reinstall every plugin or
       the scan will deadlock on stale .so's (see the caveat documented under
       item 23).

[ ] 33 Meshtastic discovery: stop treating a BARE 10c4:ea60 as a Meshtastic
       device. The VID:PID table in lib/pn-mesh-discover.c (KNOWN_DEVICES)
       matches the Heltec V3 on 10c4:ea60 -- but that is the GENERIC Silicon
       Labs CP2102 /
       CP2102N USB-UART chip, used by countless unrelated boards. In particular
       the common Sonoff/ITead Zigbee dongles (ZBDongle-P) are CP2102N ->
       10c4:ea60, so a plugged-in Zigbee coordinator shows up in the Meshtastic
       dialog as a "Heltec V3". Picking it aims the Meshtastic want_config_id
       handshake at a Z-Stack UART (root cause of the 2026-05-29 zigbee2mqtt
       CPU-spin incident: pipnode opened the shared /dev/ttyUSB and wrote
       Meshtastic frames into it; zigbee2mqtt choked on the corrupted byte
       stream).

         MITIGATED 2026-05-29 (see the in-use guard added the same day): such a
         device, while it is open by zigbee2mqtt (or anything else), now appears
         DISABLED in the list with an "in use by <proc>" note and can never be
         opened (pn_mesh_tty_in_use + TIOCEXCL in pn-mesh-serial.c). But that
         only helps WHILE the other process holds the port -- an idle, unclaimed
         CP2102 Zigbee dongle would still be mislabelled "Heltec V3" and be
         connectable.

         THE WORK (why it is hard): sysfs alone cannot disambiguate -- the
         product / manufacturer / serial strings are the chip's generic defaults
         ("CP2102 USB to UART Bridge Controller" / "Silicon Labs"), identical on
         a Heltec and a Zigbee dongle. The only reliable tell is a safe,
         read-only probe over the wire (e.g. a Meshtastic want_config_id and a
         bounded wait for a valid 0x94 0xC3 frame) -- but ANY write is exactly
         what we must NOT do to a non-Meshtastic device. Options to weigh: (a)
         mark generic-CP2102 matches as "unverified / possibly another device"
         in the UI and require an explicit user confirm before the first write;
         (b) keep a denylist of known-NON-mesh CP2102 product/serial strings;
         (c) cross-check against a system Zigbee/serial registry. None is clean;
         deferred deliberately.

[ ] 36 Stop ModemManager from probing Meshtastic serial devices. When a tty
       appears, ModemManager (root) opens it and fires AT commands -- which
       toggles DTR/RTS (rebooting the ESP32) and races pipnode's open. pipnode's
       in-use guard is a /proc scan that can't see root-owned holders, so it
       misses MM. Fix: ship a udev rule setting ID_MM_DEVICE_IGNORE=1 for the
       Meshtastic VID:PIDs (packaging step), and/or inhibit the device over MM's
       D-Bus API around open. Caveat: 10c4:ea60 is generic CP2102 (see #33); a
       blanket ignore also hides real CP2102 modems. Touches system config --
       weigh against the minimal-invasive-packaging line.

