[ ] 47 SSH credentials profile type (host-provisioned, ABI v5 PnProfileSchema /
       PnVault). Several nodes open an SSH session to a remote host and run a
       command there, but today the only "credential" they carry is a bare
       in-node "host" entry; the actual auth is whatever the desktop user's
       ambient SSH state happens to provide. Every such node builds the same
       fixed argv -- `ssh -o BatchMode=yes -o StrictHostKeyChecking=accept-new
       -o ConnectTimeout=5 <host> <cmd>` (shell plugin: pn_shell_wrap_argv in
       plugins/shell/pn-shell-host.c; host-monitoring: the same flags
       hand-rolled into each node's command string). That means: no username
       override (always the local user), no port, no chosen identity file, no
       way to reach a host that isn't already key-trusted, and host-key policy
       silently pinned to accept-new. Move the auth bits into a first-class
       "SSH Login" credentials type the user manages in the Credentials dialog,
       and give each SSH node an optional profile-ref alongside its existing
       host field. Leave the in-node host entry exactly as it is (item is
       host = WHERE, profile = HOW-TO-LOG-IN); a node with no profile keeps the
       current ambient-key behaviour for backwards compat.

       REDESIGN (2026-06-08): the credential is now HOST-BOUND and the dialog
       is driven by an auth-schema combobox. Two changes to the model above:

         (1) The host moves INTO the credential. An "SSH Login" credential must
             always name a host (hostname or IP); a hostless / generic
             credential is forbidden. The point is a permission grant: creating
             the credential is the user saying "nodes may ssh into THIS host
             with these settings", never "with these settings into any host on
             earth". So host is a required, always-visible profile field, and
             the credentials manager refuses to save an empty one. The node's
             own in-node "host" entry stays, but is consulted ONLY when the
             picker is on "Custom settings" (PN_PROFILE_REF_CUSTOM) — exactly
             the MQTT Source model: pick a profile -> host + auth come from the
             credential; pick "Custom settings" -> the node's inline host (and
             today's ambient key) are used. (Default / primary still works.)

         (2) An auth-schema combobox is the FIRST field and DRIVES which other
             fields are visible (hidden outright, not just disabled). The
             schemas (Q-confirmed 2026-06-08):
               - "passwordless simple" (DEFAULT): shows ONLY host. The user has
                 already set up an account + passwordless (agent / default-key)
                 ssh for themselves; everything else is hidden — local user,
                 port 22, no key/password, accept-new. This is today's ambient
                 login, now pinned to one host.
               - "key file": shows host, port, username, private key, key
                 passphrase, host-key policy (NO password).
               - "password": shows host, port, username, password, host-key
                 policy (NO private key / passphrase). Non-interactive feeding
                 is still the 47.5 problem; the field is reachable regardless.
             Field ORDER in the dialog (every schema uses this order, hiding the
             ones it doesn't show): auth-schema combobox, host, port, username,
             password, private key, key passphrase, host-key policy.

       This redesign reworks the schema (47.1) and the per-node wiring (47.4),
       which were built under the old "host stays in the node, profile is auth-
       only, no Custom entry" assumption; see the new sub-items 47.8–47.11.
       It needs one new core capability: conditional field visibility in
       PnProfileSchema + the credentials manager (47.8).

