   (TODO #60) Bridge Converter node -- the input-driven sibling of Bridge Quote.
       Send it an amount and it asks a real bridge what it would actually pay
       out for exactly that; the answer comes back on the message that asked,
       a network round-trip later. Bridge pricing is amount-sensitive (a flat
       network fee dominates a small swap), so a cached rate cannot honestly
       be re-scaled -- the node has to ask again for every amount, and it only
       has an amount when a message arrives. Hence no autotrigger.
       [x] 60.1 Extracted the shared provider back-ends into
             lib/pn-bridge-common.[ch]: the provider + ticker tables, the
             PnBridge GType, PulseLN's fee arithmetic, ChangeNOW's error
             vocabulary and its range lookup. No PnNode in the file -- every
             function takes a PnBridgeRequest and fills a PnBridgeResult, so
             the parse path can be driven from a test with a captured body.
             pn-bridge-quote.c shrank from 1766 to ~1150 lines and now folds a
             result into its cache instead of parsing inline; its 113-check
             suite passed unchanged, which is what made the refactor safe to
             do first.
       [x] 60.2 lib/pn-bridge-converter.[ch] (core tier). has_input=TRUE.
             receive() reads data.value as the amount, parks the message
             itself, and kicks the inherited auto-trigger worker; the trigger
             override moves the parked request into flight and chains to
             PnHttp::trigger; the reply is stamped onto that same message, so
             upstream fields survive the round trip the way PnRate's do.
             The auto-trigger machinery is used purely as a worker thread --
             an idle tick finds the pending slot empty and returns, so the
             node never polls.
       [x] 60.3 At most one request in flight. A newer amount replaces a
             parked one (logged at INFO) rather than queueing behind it:
             a converter fed faster than the network answers should quote the
             newest amount, not work through a backlog of stale ones. That
             also bounds the request rate to one per round-trip whatever the
             input does. A message with no numeric data.value, or one <= 0, is
             failed locally and emitted with success=FALSE rather than
             spending a request on a rejection.
       [x] 60.4 `period` is meaningless on a node with no schedule, so it is
             pinned to 30 s by a notify hook and hidden from the dialog with a
             PnSettingsSchema row carrying PN_ROW_FLAG_HIDDEN. It survives
             only as the per-request socket timeout PnHttp derives from it.
             `amount` is published when the request goes OUT, not when it
             comes back, so a failed conversion still says what was asked --
             and that doubles as the headless test seam.
       [x] 60.5 Never having converted anything is NOT an error state here.
             Bridge Quote paints itself red while its cache is deprecated,
             which is what made TODO #59.9 possible; a converter with nothing
             wired into it yet is idle, not broken, so has-error is only set
             after a conversion actually failed. No settle-idle needed.
       [x] 60.6 gui tier lib/pn-bridge-converter-gui.[ch] (read-only rows for
             every result field, icon+ticker combos for from/to), registration
             in pn-node-factory.c / pn-gui.c / lib/Makefile.am (4 lists) /
             data/Makefile.am / tests/unit/Makefile.am.
       [x] 60.7 Tests (tests/unit/test-pn-bridge-converter.c, 83 checks,
             headless). Three seams keep it off the network: autostart=FALSE
             (no worker thread), an empty url (a hand-driven trigger stops
