[ ] 77 Burglar Alarm node (PnBurglarAlarm) -- the first CONTROLLER: a
       self-contained state machine that runs a whole appliance rather than
       transforming one message. Five inputs that mean different things and five
       outputs that speak at different times, which is what sets the new
       "Controllers" palette group apart from the Filters groups. Keypad ->
       Panel -> LCD + siren + voice + radio is a working intruder alarm.

       [x] 77.1 Core lib/pn-burglar-alarm.[ch] (GTK-free, no gui tier -- the node
             is header-only and the six properties render in the generic
             settings dialog). Logic seam pn_burglar_alarm_press() / _submit() /
             _set_zone() / _trip_zone() / _tick() so a headless test can walk a
             whole burglary without building messages or waiting out a real
             delay. _tick() deliberately does NOT touch the node's own 1 Hz
             timer, so a test driving it directly never arms a main-loop source.
       [x] 77.2 Inputs: 0 keypad (data.key, one keystroke -- what the Keypad's
             Decimal pad emits; failing that a whole code in data.output, so
             MQTT can arm remotely), 1 delayed (the entry/exit route), 2..4
             zone 1-3 (instant). A numeric data.value sets a zone's HELD state
             (a door contact); NO numeric value is a MOMENTARY trip (a PIR) that
             leaves the zone closed -- the difference is what decides whether
             the panel can arm.
       [x] 77.3 Zone names ARE the input names (pn_node_get_input_name), so a
             rename on the node's Inputs tab renames the zone in the readout,
             the spoken sentence and the sms. No bespoke name properties.
       [x] 77.4 Outputs: 0 alarm (the siren, 0.0/1.0 + data.cause), 1 display
             (2-4 LCD lines in data.output, countdown seconds in data.value),
             2 armed (0.0/1.0), 3 speech (one spoken sentence per event, for
             Text to Speech), 4 sms (one written sentence per IMPORTANT event,
             for Meshtastic). Every message carries data.state. alarm/armed are
             written only on a change; sms on arming is opt-in (a radio message
             per arming is a lot of radio).
       [x] 77.5 States disarmed -> exit -> armed -> entry -> alarm. Arming is
             refused while an INSTANT zone is held open (NOT READY names it);
             the delayed zone may be open -- that is the door being left
             through. Zones are ignored during the exit delay but re-read the
             moment arming bites. The siren cuts off after siren-time while the
             state STAYS alarm, remembering the cause, until a code disarms.
       [x] 77.6 The panel paints itself red on the worksheet while tripped, via
             the generic node error state (TODO #71-era pn_node_set_has_error)
             rather than a bespoke icon swap.
       [x] 77.7 Startup announce (g_idle, Knob/Switch pattern): display, armed=0
             and alarm=0 once on load, so a wired LCD is not left blank and a
             wired siren is told to be quiet -- but SILENT on speech and sms,
             which would otherwise talk to the room every time the worksheet is
             opened. The state itself is not serialized: a reloaded worksheet
             comes up disarmed, the only safe assumption about a house nobody
             was watching.
       [x] 77.8 Matrix57 grew from 1-2 to 1-4 lines so the display output has a
             real 20x4 target (the request was "a two line or a four line LCD").
             Past two rows the NODE grows (PN_M57_ROW_HEIGHT, 45 px per extra
             row) instead of the dots shrinking; 1 and 2 keep the historical
             90 px body so no existing worksheet moves. The panel-applet mirror
             follows the same range but keeps its panel-row height.
       [x] 77.9 Factory registration under the new "Controllers" category, help
             page PnBurglarAlarm.html, pipnode-nodes skill entry (the new
             reference/controllers.md + the Matrix57 line-count update).
       [x] 77.10 Unit tests tests/unit/test-pn-burglar-alarm.c -- the whole
             state machine driven through the logic seam (126 checks): exit and
             entry delays, zero-delay shortcuts, wrong/empty codes, NOT READY,
             held vs momentary zones, the siren cut-off and its no-cut-off mode,
             the 2/3/4-line readouts, the startup announce (and its silence on
             speech/sms), sms gating, and the message shapes on all five
             outputs.
       [x] 77.11 Example worksheet examples/controls-and-logic/burglar-alarm.json
             (Keypad + four Switches -> Panel -> 20x4 Matrix57, two LEDs, Text to
             Speech, and a Text View standing in for a Meshtastic node).
       [ ] 77.12 GUI check by hand: type 1234 # on the pad and watch the LCD
             count the exit delay down to ARMED; flip the Front door switch and
             confirm the entry delay counts instead of the siren; flip a window
             and confirm the siren LED blinks, the node paints red, and the
             Text View shows the sms text.
       [ ] 77.13 Candidate follow-ups, deliberately NOT implemented: a keypad
             lockout / tamper alarm after N wrong codes (real panels do this); a
             duress code that disarms but still texts; per-zone "chime while
             disarmed"; a 24-hour zone that trips even when disarmed; and a
             partial ("night") arm that watches only some zones. The code is
             stored in the worksheet as PLAIN TEXT -- a certified panel would
             want it in the PnVault (see #27), which is the one thing here that
             is a real limitation rather than a missing convenience.

[ ] 78 Keypad Door Lock node (PnDoorLock) -- the second CONTROLLER, and the
       small sibling of the #77 alarm panel: a code pad that drives an electric
       strike. Where the alarm asks "is the house armed", the lock asks WHO is
       at the door -- it holds several codes, one per person, and names the
       person on every unlock. Keypad -> Lock -> Tasmota relay + LCD is a
       working door station. Keeping the "who" here is also what lets #77 stay
       a one-code panel instead of growing a user list it does not need.

       [ ] 78.1 Core lib/pn-door-lock.[ch] (GTK-free, no gui tier -- the
             properties render in the generic settings dialog). Logic seam
             pn_door_lock_press() / _submit() / _set_door() / _request_exit() /
             _tick() / _reset(), plus the read seam (_get_state(),
             _get_strike(), _get_user(), _get_remaining(),
             _get_display_text()), so a headless test walks a whole shift
             without building messages or waiting out a real delay -- exactly
             the shape #75.1 and #77.1 settled on. _tick() must not touch the
             node's own 1 Hz timer.
       [ ] 78.2 Inputs: 0 keypad (data.key, one keystroke -- what the #76
             Decimal or Phone pad emits; failing that a WHOLE code in
             data.output, so an MQTT feed or a card reader can present a
             credential), 1 exit (request-to-exit -- the button on the inside,
             unlocks with no code and names nobody), 2 door (the contact; a
             numeric data.value is the held open/closed state, the door-contact
             half of #77.2).
       [ ] 78.3 Outputs: 0 strike (0.0/1.0, held for unlock-time seconds --
             wire it to an LED or a Tasmota relay), 1 display (LCD lines in
             data.output, countdown seconds in data.value, for a Matrix57),
             2 event -- ONE message per decision carrying data.user (the person,
             "" when nobody), data.state and a human sentence in data.output.
             Deliberately NOT #77's three-way speech/sms split: one event tap
             feeds a Text to Speech, a Logger or a Meshtastic node, and a Filter
             on data.state picks what each of them deserves. The typed code
             NEVER leaves the node -- not in a message, not in the display.
       [ ] 78.4 States locked -> open -> ajar, plus lockout and forced.
             LOCKED   watching; typing builds a code.
             OPEN     a code (or the exit button) matched: strike energised,
                      counting unlock-time down. Relocks on the timer, or
                      early once the door has been opened AND closed again --
                      the "one person, one unlock" rule every real strike has.
             AJAR     the door was left open past ajar-time: an event, and the
                      node paints red, but the strike is already back off.
             LOCKOUT  after wrong-tries wrong codes the pad goes dead for
                      lockout-time seconds and emits one event. The count
                      resets on any successful unlock.
             FORCED   the contact opened while locked and nothing unlocked it:
                      one event, and it stays forced until a valid code is
                      typed. This is the lock's own break-in detection and it
                      is core, not a follow-up -- a lock that cannot tell a
                      forced door from an opened one is a doorbell.
       [ ] 78.5 Where the codes live. Primary: the PnVault (#27). A new
             core-owned profile type "door-code" (pn-door-code-profile.c,
             registered next to mqtt-broker / ssh-login / ethereum-address in
             pn-node-factory.c) whose profile NAME is the person and whose one
             SECRET field is the code. The node holds no profile-ref: it
             enumerates every profile of the type with pn_vault_list_profiles()
             and matches the typed digits against all of them -- the first node
             that uses the vault as a SET rather than as one named credential.
             Re-read on PnVault::changed, so adding a person does not need a
             reload. This is the answer to the plain-text limitation #77.13
             admits to.
       [ ] 78.6 Secondary: a plain "codes" property, PROM-contents style, one
             "<name> <code>" pair per line, merged with whatever the vault
             holds. It exists so the example worksheet and the unit tests have
             working codes without provisioning a vault first -- and its tooltip
             and help page must say plainly that it is stored in the worksheet
             as clear text, with the vault as the upgrade.
       [ ] 78.7 Typing rules: "#" or "=" submits, "*" or "C" clears what has
             been typed. The display echoes stars, never the digits. An
             inter-digit timeout (property, default ~5 s) drops a half-typed
             code, so the next person does not inherit three stray digits.
             Optional auto-submit: check after every keystroke once the typed
             length equals some stored code's length, which is how a real pad
             with no "#" key behaves.
       [ ] 78.8 A duress code -- the second of #77.13's follow-ups, and it lands
             here rather than there. Per person (a second secret field on the
             profile): it unlocks exactly as the normal code does, the display
             and the strike show nothing unusual, but the event carries
             data.duress = TRUE so a Filter can text somebody quietly.
       [ ] 78.9 Startup announce (g_idle, the Knob/Switch pattern): display and
             strike = 0 once on load, so a wired LCD is not left blank and a
             wired strike is explicitly told to stay locked. Silent on the event
             output. The state is NOT serialized -- a reloaded worksheet comes
             up locked, the only safe assumption.
       [ ] 78.10 Paints red through the generic node error state
             (pn_node_set_has_error, #71-era) while in lockout, ajar or forced,
             as #77.6 does while tripped.
       [ ] 78.11 Factory registration under "Controllers", help page
             PnDoorLock.html, a DoorCodeProfile.html for the new profile type
             (pn_profile_schema_set_help_page, as #27's credentials work set
             up), and the pipnode-nodes skill entry in reference/controllers.md.
       [ ] 78.12 Unit tests tests/unit/test-pn-door-lock.c -- the state machine
             driven through the logic seam: right and wrong codes, several
             people and the name on the event, the exit button naming nobody,
             relock on timer vs relock on door-closed, ajar, the lockout count
             and its reset, forced entry, the duress flag, the inter-digit
             timeout, auto-submit on and off, the star echo (and that no code
             ever appears in an emitted message), the startup announce and the
             message shapes on all three outputs.
       [ ] 78.13 Example worksheet examples/controls-and-logic/door-lock.json
             (Keypad + a Switch for the door contact + a Switch for the exit
             button -> Lock -> Matrix57 + an LED for the strike + a Text View on
             the event output), with the codes in the plain property and a
             Comment pointing at the vault.
       [ ] 78.14 GUI check by hand: type one person's code and watch the LED
             energise, the LCD name them and the strike drop after the unlock
             time; flip the door switch open and closed mid-unlock and confirm
             it relocks early; type three wrong codes and confirm LOCKOUT, the
             red node and the dead pad; flip the door open while locked and
             confirm FORCED.
       [ ] 78.15 Candidate follow-ups, deliberately NOT implemented: time-of-day
             validity (a code that only works 07:00-19:00, which wants a Daily
             Timer wired into a fourth input rather than a schedule property);
             one-time or guest codes with a use count, which would mean writing
             back to the vault; an RFID/Wiegand credential as a first-class
             input rather than a whole code on the keypad input; a lockdown
             input that refuses every code; and the obvious pairing -- a valid
             unlock disarming the #77 panel, which today is just the event
             output wired into the alarm's keypad input, and should stay that
             way until it demonstrably is not enough.

[ ] 79 A wave-optics teaching sheet -- single slit -> double slit ->
       diffraction grating, all three out of ONE formula and one Calculator 2.
       The optics sibling of the Uniswap AMM example
       (examples/crypto/uniswap-v2.json), built on the same skeleton: knob
       column -> Ramp -> element-wise core -> LED panels and plots. Where that
       sheet taught a RATIO, this one teaches a PRODUCT:

           I/I0 = [sin(al)/al]^2 * [sin(N*be)/(N*sin(be))]^2
                  al = pi*w*s/lam     be = pi*d*s/lam     s = sin(theta)

       The first factor is what ONE slit does (the envelope), the second is
       what N point sources do (the interference comb), and the pattern on the
       wall is their product. Winding the slit-count knob walks the whole
       syllabus without rewiring anything: N=1 collapses the comb to 1 and
       leaves the bare single slit, N=2 is the textbook double slit, and N
       large sharpens the comb into a grating's needle-thin orders while the
       envelope above it never moves.

       [ ] 79.1 The file. examples/Compute/diffraction.json, beside
             projectile.json and lissajous.json -- Compute is where the vector
             machinery already lives. Five-band layout copied from the AMM
             sheet: title Comment, four section Comments, then knob column ->
             sweep -> core -> readouts -> plots.
       [ ] 79.2 GOTCHA found while planning: examples/Compute is NOT in
             examples/Makefile.am -- nor are Clocks, meshtastic or
             panel-applet-ideas -- so nothing in it has ever been installed.
             Add a computedir/compute_DATA block and sweep up the five
             existing files with it, or the new sheet ships only to people
             who clone the repo. Worth doing for the other three dirs in the
             same commit; they are the same one-block fix.
       [ ] 79.3 Six knobs, every one a real optical-bench control. Slit width
             w (1..25 um, 1 um a notch, default 8); slit separation d (5..125
             um, 5 um a notch, default 40); wavelength lam (0.40..0.76 um, the
             visible band, default 0.55); slit count N (1..25, one a notch,
             default 2); screen distance L (0.25..6.25 m, which turns angles
             into millimetres on the wall); and view width k (0.5..12.5),
             how many envelope lobes to draw. Pick every range so one wheel
             notch is a round number -- 24 detents to a full sweep.
       [ ] 79.4 The sweep runs over s = sin(theta), NOT theta. Every term in
             the formula only ever sees sin(theta), so sweeping it directly
             saves a sine per element and leaves the x axis linear in position
             on the screen. Ramp from = -smax to = +smax with
             smax = k*lam/w, so the view auto-scales to the physics rather
             than to a fixed angle: one Calculator 2 (k, lam, w) computes
             smax and feeds "to", a one-line Calculator negating it feeds
             "from". 1024 samples, EVEN on purpose (see 79.6).
       [ ] 79.5 One Calculator 2, five named inputs (s, w, d, lam, N),
             publishing THREE vectors as message members:
                 al   = 3.14159265 * w * s / lam + 1e-9
                 be   = 3.14159265 * d * s / lam + 1e-9
                 env  = (sin(al)/al) * (sin(al)/al)
                 comb = sin(N*be) / (N * sin(be))
                 intf = comb * comb
                 tot  = env * intf
             Normalising the comb by N is what keeps all three on the same
             0..1 vertical scale, so the sub-plots in 79.7 are directly
             comparable. NOTE: there is no pow() and ^ is XOR, not power --
             square by multiplying, as above.
       [ ] 79.6 Two removable singularities, both 0/0 and both fatal to the
             element they land on: sin(al)/al at al=0 (dead centre) and
             sin(N*be)/sin(be) at every principal maximum be = m*pi. The 1e-9
             offsets in 79.5 are the fix -- near zero sin(N*e)/sin(e) tends to
             N smoothly, so the offset costs nothing and removes the NaN.
             Belt and braces: an even sample count means no sample sits
             exactly on s = 0. Same class of bug as the AMM sheet's dy/dx, and
             it wants the same answer: reshape so the division never sees 0/0.
       [ ] 79.7 THE SUB-PLOTS -- the centrepiece, and the reason the core
             publishes three vectors instead of one. Three PnPlot panels
             stacked in one column, sharing the sweep so their x axes line up:
                 envelope   value-key env    what ONE slit does
                 comb       value-key intf   what N point sources do
                 pattern    value-key tot    the product, what the wall shows
             Read top to bottom they ARE the derivation, and the third is
             visibly the first two multiplied. PnPlot is single-series and
             swallows a whole vector per message, so each panel is one node
             with no extra wiring. Lines, show-grid on, y-from-zero on so the
             three share a floor.
       [ ] 79.8 A PnOscilloscope beside them tracing tot against s. The Plot
             panels' x axis is a bucket index; the scope's is the real
             sin(theta), so it is the one that reads in physical units -- and
             the green CRT is the closest thing to looking at the actual
             screen. Check whether it wants a fixed frame or auto-fit (79.11).
       [ ] 79.9 LED readouts, each a one-line Calculator picking a field:
             fringe spacing lam*L/d (mm), envelope first minimum lam*L/w (mm),
             highest visible order floor(d/lam), principal-maximum angular
             width lam/(N*d) -- the resolving power -- and 2*d/w, the number
             of fringes fitting under the central envelope. That last one is
             the shape parameter, the d/w ratio this sheet is really about, so
             give it the Dial (0..40) as well. A Reduce in sum mode over tot
             gives total power through the slits for a Numeric.
       [ ] 79.10 A MISSING-ORDERS lamp, the nicest bit of physics on the
             sheet: when d/w is a whole number the envelope's zero lands
             exactly on a principal maximum and that order vanishes. Detect it
             with the mask idiom -- abs(d/w - floor(d/w + 0.5)) < 0.02 -- into
             an LED in Blink Fast. Wind d with w fixed and the lamp ticks
             every time an order dies. Worth a Matrix57 "bench card" too: slit
             width, separation, wavelength and screen distance on four lines.
       [ ] 79.11 Before shipping, run the scale-invariance check the AMM sheet
             taught us the hard way: for EVERY knob, confirm it changes the
             SHAPE of the plotted curve and not merely its scale, because an
             auto-fitting axis silently cancels a pure scaling and the panel
             then looks frozen. Expected here: w, d and N all bend the
             pattern (via d/w and N), lam and w both set smax so the view
             tracks them, and L is pure unit conversion that must therefore
             show up ONLY in the millimetre readouts, never in a plot. Prove
             it by dumping the vectors headless at two knob settings and
             comparing the normalised traces, not by eye.
       [ ] 79.12 Comments carrying the teaching text, as on the AMM sheet: the
             formula and the product idea up top, then one box per band. Say
             explicitly that the envelope belongs to the single slit and the
             comb to the array, because that split is the whole lesson and is
             invisible unless someone writes it down.
       [ ] 79.13 Verify headless with ./src/pipnode-run -t 3 before any GUI
             work: no CRITICALs, no NaN in the three vectors at the extremes
             of every knob (N=1 and N=25, w=1 and w=25), and check the node
             boxes do not overlap. Then the GUI check by hand: wind N from 1
             to 25 and watch the comb sharpen under a stationary envelope;
             wind w and watch the envelope close in while the fringe spacing
             holds; wind d and watch the fringes crowd and the missing-order
             lamp tick.

[ ] 80 A FIGURE node -- a little drawing language painted live in the
       node's client area, where every number in the drawing is an
       expression over the node's inputs. The point is worksheets that
       look like the plates in a 19th-century physics book -- a lever, a
       pendulum, a lens with its rays, a Carnot cylinder, a Wheatstone
       bridge -- except the picture moves, because the numbers behind it
       arrive down a wire. A program is one statement per line -- this
       one is the reference specimen, a lever tilted by its input `a`,
       and the language was chosen against it (80.1):

           view -60, -25, 60, 35
           color "#202020"
           width 2

           # the beam, tilted by the input angle
           dx = 50 * cos(a)
           dy = 50 * sin(a)
           line -dx, -dy, dx, dy

           # fulcrum
           fill "#808080"
           poly 0,-2, -8,-14, 8,-14
           nofill

           # weight hanging off the left end
           rect -dx-6, -dy-16, 12, 10

           text -dx, -dy+6, "A"
           text  dx,  dy+6, "B"
           text 0, 22, "%.1f deg", a * 57.2958

       This is deliberately NOT another plotting node: PnPlot, PnGraph
       and PnOscilloscope already plot data series. This one draws the
       APPARATUS, not the curve -- the thing the data is about.

       [x] 80.1 DECIDED (2026-09-21): pipnode's own verb-per-line
             grammar, taking PIC's verb names and HPGL's pen-state
             model. Surveyed first: PIC (Kernighan's troff figure
             language, alive today as pikchr and dpic), HPGL (the
             pen-plotter standard -- PU/PD, PA/PR, CI, SP, LT), BASIC's
             graphics statements, Logo turtle, SVG path data, TikZ and
             MetaPost.

             Why not pikchr, the closest thing to an off-the-shelf
             answer: its power is IMPLICIT LAYOUT -- direction chains,
             `last circle`, `A.se` -- and we would use none of it,
             because our positions come from expressions instead. It
             has no rotation either, so the tilted beam in any
             mechanics figure means computing endpoints by hand, which
             is this grammar with more keywords bolted on; and its
             expression language is its own, not our calculator. A
             few-thousand-line grammar for the parts we would throw
             away. HPGL loses on legibility alone -- unreadable is
             fatal for a node whose output is teaching material -- but
             its pen-state machine is the right one and we take it.
             Logo is wrong as the base (most of a physics plate is
             "circle at (x,y)", not "forward 50") but `turn` and
             `forward` are two cheap verbs to add later on top of the
             pen position 80.6 already keeps.

             Two consequences settled here, so they do not get
             reopened: (a) STYLING IS PEN STATE, not per-shape option
             lists -- the Tk-canvas/SVG style `line x1,y1,x2,y2
             -fill red -width 2` would mix keywords into an argument
             list that is otherwise pure expressions, and every line
             would carry its own boilerplate; (b) the resemblance to
             PIC is lineage, not accident, and the help page says so.

             Three facts about the parser we are building on, checked
             rather than assumed, that fall out of the choice:
             the lexer knows NO comma and NO string literal, so the
             statement splitter must handle both before handing
             fragments to pn_expr_parser_parse() -- split on commas at
             paren depth 0, since single-argument calls are all that
             exists today but atan2/min/max would break a naive split;
             parse errors already carry a column ("expected ')' at
             position %d"), so 80.10's "line 7, position 12" costs
             nothing to produce; and everything else we need --
             precedence, the function table, `name = expr` statements,
             scalar-or-vector values -- is already in PnExprParser and
             PnVarStore.
       [x] 80.2 DECIDED (2026-09-21): the grammar, kept boring on
             purpose. One statement per line, and that is the whole
             shape of it:

                 program    := line*
                 line       := blank | comment | assignment | statement
                 comment    := "#" ... end of line
                 assignment := IDENT "=" <calculator expression>
                 statement  := VERB [ arg ("," arg)* ]
                 arg        := STRING | <calculator expression>
                 STRING     := text in double quotes, \" and \\ escaped

             An assignment line is handed WHOLE to
             pn_expr_parser_parse(), which already returns an ASSIGN
             node, and pn_var_store_evaluate_value() already performs
             the binding -- no new code, and vector assignments come
             along free. So `r = sqrt(dx*dx + dy*dy)` works because the
             calculator works, not because the figure language does
             anything.

             The rules, each one a decision rather than an accident:

             1. Verb or assignment? The first token is an identifier; if
                the next non-space character is "=" and not "==", the
                line is an assignment, otherwise that identifier is a
                verb. A variable may therefore legally be called `line`.
                The help page says: do not.
             2. Verbs are case-insensitive (`Line` == `line`); variable
                names are case-sensitive, because the expression lexer
                already is.
             3. WE split the arguments, not the expression parser: on
                commas only, at paren depth 0, outside quotes. Depth
                matters even though every function today takes one
                argument -- the day atan2/min/max lands, a naive split
                breaks silently rather than loudly.
             4. Comment stripping is string-aware for the same reason: a
                "#" inside quotes is a character, not a comment.
             5. Continuation: a line whose last non-comment character is
                a comma continues on the next line. A long `poly` wants
                this; a backslash continuation would be a second
                mechanism for one job.
             6. Leading whitespace is free -- indent to group a figure
                into its parts. No semicolons, no blocks, no nesting;
                80.18 gets to revisit that exactly once.
             7. QUOTED MEANS LITERAL, UNQUOTED MEANS EXPRESSION, in
                every argument position, not just the colours that
                forced the rule in 80.1: `text x, y, "A", "centre"`,
                `dash "dot"`, `color "#202020"`. One rule, no per-verb
                exceptions to memorise.
             8. Fixed arity per verb, checked at parse time. The one
                variadic is `poly` -- an even argument count, at least
                three points.
             9. Unknown verb, wrong arity, unterminated string: parse
                errors. Parse the WHOLE program and collect them all,
                then report the first with its line and column and say
                how many there were ("3 errors, first on line 7"). A
                program with any error draws nothing -- see 80.10.
            10. An empty program is a blank figure, not an error.
            11. `view` is pen state like everything else: executed in
                sequence, applying to what follows, with the default
                window in force before it. One less special case than
                making it a global.
            12. FREE-VARIABLE PRE-BIND -- needed, and found by reading
                PnVarStore rather than by assuming: an unbound name
                raises PN_VAR_STORE_ERROR_UNKNOWN_VARIABLE, it does not
                evaluate to 0, so 80.8's promise that an unwired node
                still draws needs machinery. At parse time walk the
                ASTs and collect the program's free identifiers; before
                each frame bind to 0 every one that no input and no
                earlier assignment supplies. Cheap, and it hands the
                dialog a list of what the program expects.
            13. Per-frame hygiene: pn_var_store_clear() before every
                frame, or last frame's assignments leak into this one.
            14. A vector argument raises the type-mismatch error today
                ("a vector reached a scalar sink"). Leave it an error in
                v1 -- and make exactly that the seam 80.16 replaces with
                the frame indexer.
       [x] 80.3 DECIDED (2026-09-21): reuse the calculator language
             verbatim -- PnExprParser to parse each argument, PnVarStore
             to evaluate it. No second grammar and NO SECOND FUNCTION
             TABLE: the moment the figure node grows its own `atan2`,
             the language has stopped being the calculator's and the two
             drift forever. What that decision actually buys and costs,
             read out of the code rather than assumed:

             (a) THE DISPLAY LIST. Parsing happens once, when the
                 program property is set, producing an array of
                 { verb, source line, arguments }, each argument being a
                 string literal, a folded constant, or an AST owned by
                 the entry and freed with pn_expr_node_free(). Nothing
                 in it is GTK-aware, so it lives in the core half
                 (80.9) and the painter merely walks it. Re-parse only
                 when the property changes; everything runs on the main
                 loop, so no locking.
             (b) CONSTANT FOLDING, free of charge. An argument whose AST
                 has no free identifiers is evaluated once at parse time
                 and kept as a number. Most of a figure is constants --
                 the `view`, the widths, every fulcrum coordinate -- so
                 the per-frame walk shrinks to the handful of arguments
                 that actually move. The identifier walker that rule
                 80.2.12 needs anyway is the whole implementation.
             (c) BINDING PRECEDENCE, which matters precisely because
                 80.2.12 zero-fills what is left over. The order is:
                 constants (pi, e) -> input members -> the animation
                 variables frame and t (80.17) -> assignments as they
                 execute -> zero-fill of anything still unbound. Get it
                 wrong and `pi` silently becomes 0 -- a figure that
                 still draws, just wrongly, which is the worst kind.
             (d) THERE IS NO `pi`. Verified: PnVarStore binds no
                 constants whatever, which is why TODO #79.5 writes
                 3.14159265 out by hand. The Figure node pre-binds `pi`
                 and `e` itself as step one of (c) rather than blocking
                 on a core change -- but the smell is real and the fix
                 belongs in PnVarStore, where every Calculator would
                 inherit it.
             (e) THE FUNCTION TABLE IS TEN UNARY FUNCTIONS: sin, cos,
                 tan, log, log10, exp, sqrt, abs, floor, ceil. No
                 asin/acos/atan, no atan2, no min/max, no hypot, no
                 round, no sign, no pow -- and `^` is XOR, so squaring
                 is a multiplication (the trap TODO #79.5 hit). For
                 mechanics figures atan2(dy,dx) is the absence that will
                 hurt first. Multi-argument calls mean changing BOTH
                 pn-expr-parser.c (parse_factor_body takes exactly one
                 expression between the parens) and pn-var-store.c (the
                 table is typed UnaryFn), in shared core, for every
                 Calculator in every worksheet -- so it got its own
                 TODO number rather than a quiet edit buried in a
                 drawing node: see #81, which also carries the missing
                 constants from (d). Until that lands, figures spell
                 atan2 out by hand and pre-bind their own pi.
             (f) VECTORS ALREADY BROADCAST -- and the rules are NOT the
                 ones 80.16 assumed. From pn-var-store.c and
                 tests/unit/test-pn-var-store.c: scalar OP vector
                 broadcasts the scalar; vector OP vector is elementwise
                 but TRUNCATED TO THE SHORTER operand; unary functions
                 map element by element; and a COMPARISON REDUCES a
                 vector operand to a single 0/1 scalar. Two consequences
                 for the animation work, written down now instead of
                 being discovered in a debugger: the frame count must be
                 the SHORTEST vector argument, not the longest, or the
                 figure disagrees with its own arithmetic; and a
                 per-frame conditional cannot be written as a comparison
                 against a vector, because the comparison collapses
                 before the frame indexer ever sees it.
       [x] 80.4 DECIDED (2026-09-21): coordinates.
             `view xmin, ymin, xmax, ymax` declares the user-unit
             window; Y POINTS UP, because this is a physics figure and
             not a screen, and that sentence goes in the help page in
             bold because it is the first thing everyone gets wrong.
             The window is fitted into the client rectangle preserving
             aspect and centred, so the mapping is:

                 ex = xmax - xmin        ey = ymax - ymin
                 s  = min (w / |ex|, h / |ey|)
                 sx =  s * |ex| / ex     sy = -s * |ey| / ey
                 ox = x + (w - s*|ex|) / 2
                 oy = y + (h - s*|ey|) / 2
                 X  = ox + (u - xmin) * sx
                 Y  = oy + s*|ey| + (v - ymin) * sy

             (a) THE FLIP DOES NOT GO IN THE CAIRO MATRIX. Map the
                 points ourselves, as above, and leave the CTM alone. A
                 negative-y CTM mirrors every glyph the `text` verb
                 draws, and an anisotropic one makes line width depend
                 on direction -- two classic bugs bought for nothing,
                 since the multiply is three instructions.
             (b) WIDTHS, DASHES AND FONT SIZES ARE USER UNITS, scaled
                 by s at draw time like everything else. That is what
                 makes the zoom overlay enlarge the figure like a
                 photograph instead of leaving spidery hairlines on a
                 big plate. Clamp the stroked width to a minimum of
                 about 0.75 device pixels so a fine line never vanishes
                 at rest.
             (c) THE DEFAULT WINDOW IS `0, 0, 100, 100` -- fixed, round
                 and documented, NOT auto-fitted to the drawing's
                 bounding box. An auto window breathes as the values
                 change, which turns every animation into an
                 accidental zoom and makes a figure impossible to
                 compare with itself. A `viewfit` verb can be added
                 later if sketching really wants one.
             (d) Reversed bounds are legal and flip that axis -- it
                 costs nothing and someone will want x increasing
                 leftwards. A DEGENERATE window (zero extent, NaN,
                 infinite) is a runtime value rather than a program
                 bug, and a knob passing through zero must not latch
                 the node red, so a bad `view` is skipped and the
                 previous window stands -- the same rule 80.10 applies
                 to any non-finite statement.
             (e) WE CLIP, because the worksheet does not: checked --
                 pn-worksheet.c calls paint_plot with the rectangle and
                 no clip of its own, and PnPlot saves, rectangles and
                 clips for itself. Do the same, or one stray coordinate
                 paints across the whole worksheet.
             (f) Fill the client rectangle with a background-colour
                 property before the program runs, exactly as PnPlot
                 does, so the letterbox bars are simply background and
                 an empty figure still reads as a deliberate area.
             (g) Set PnNodeClass.paint_plot_zoom_keep_aspect to %TRUE:
                 the figure already preserves its own aspect, so a
                 stretched overlay would only grow the letterbox bars.
             (h) Aspect preserved and centred by default, with a
                 "stretch" boolean property for anyone who wants the
                 drawing distorted to fill the card.
             (i) The transform belongs to the CORE half, not the
                 painter -- follow pn_plot_get_paint_state(): core
                 evaluates the display list and computes the mapping
                 for a given rectangle, the gui half walks it and
                 strokes. That is what lets 80.12 assert device
                 coordinates in a headless test with no cairo anywhere.
       [x] 80.5 DECIDED (2026-09-21): pen state, HPGL-style -- the
             figure reads as a sequence of pen changes and strokes, and
             every setting persists until something changes it. THE
             WHOLE STATE VECTOR, written down once because it is the
             thing the core evaluates into and the painter consumes:
             stroke colour, fill colour, fill on/off, line width, dash
             pattern and its scale, font size, TEXT ALIGNMENT (added by
             80.7), pen position (80.6) and the current view (80.2 rule
             11). Nothing else is stateful.

             The verbs:
                 color  <colour>          stroke colour
                 fill   <colour>          fill colour, and fill ON
                 nofill                   fill OFF
                 width  w                 stroke width, user units
                 dash   "style" [, scale] pattern, user units
                 font   size              text size, user units
                 align  "h" [, "v"]       text anchoring (80.7)
             where <colour> is ONE quoted literal or THREE-or-FOUR
             expressions (r, g, b [, a] in 0..1). That is the one
             two-form arity in the language and it clarifies rather
             than contradicts 80.2 rule 8: a colour ARGUMENT has two
             spellings, every other verb has a fixed count.

             (a) COLOUR PARSING IS ALREADY WRITTEN, and it is core:
                 pn_color_parse() in lib/pn-color.c is GTK-free and
                 takes `#rgb`, `#rgba`, `#rrggbb`, `#rrggbbaa`,
                 `rgb(...)`, `rgba(...)` and `transparent`, locale
                 independently, and PnColor is byte-compatible with
                 GdkRGBA so the painter casts for free. Use it
                 verbatim; the figure node must not grow a colour
                 parser of its own any more than it grows a function
                 table.
             (b) It parses no NAMES, though -- checked, there is no
                 name table, only hex and the rgb() forms. Since 80.1
                 justified quoted colour literals partly on legibility,
                 add a short name table (black, white, red, green,
                 blue, yellow, cyan, magenta, grey/gray, orange, brown)
                 TO pn-color.c, not to the figure node: it is a strict
                 superset of what that function accepts today, no
                 existing input changes meaning, and pn_color_to_string
                 still emits rgb()/rgba() so the save format is
                 untouched. One table, whole project. It is a core
                 change all the same, so it lands with its cases added
                 to the existing tests/unit/test-pn-color.c rather than
                 riding in on a drawing node's commit.
             (c) DEFAULT WIDTH IS 1 USER UNIT, and `width 0` means a
                 device hairline -- the PostScript convention, and the
                 escape hatch from 80.4(b)'s rule that widths scale
                 with the view. Everything else scales like a
                 photograph; a hairline stays a hairline on purpose.
                 Note for the specimen reader: its `width 2` is two
                 USER units, a deliberately bold beam, not two pixels.
             (d) DASH PATTERNS in user units, multiplied by the
                 optional scale and then by s at draw time:
                 "solid" = [], "dot" = [0.5, 1.5], "dash" = [3, 2],
                 "dashdot" = [3, 2, 0.5, 2].
             (e) FILL THEN STROKE. A shape drawn with fill on is filled
                 and then outlined in the stroke colour
                 (cairo_fill_preserve + stroke) -- which is why `color`
                 and `fill` are separate state and neither sets the
                 other: the specimen's fulcrum is a grey triangle with
                 a dark outline, and that is the look the whole style
                 depends on.
             (f) ROUND CAP AND ROUND JOIN, fixed, no verb. Cairo
                 defaults to a butt cap and a mitre join; mitre spikes
                 on the sharp corner of a `poly` are ugly and surprising
                 and nobody drawing a lever wants to think about it.
             (g) PEN STATE RESETS AT THE START OF EVERY FRAME to those
                 defaults. Persisting it across frames would make frame
                 N depend on frame N-1, which is exactly the bug class
                 80.16's film has no way to debug.
             (h) A BONUS FROM 80.2 RULE 7, worth naming because it is
                 free: an unknown dash style, an unknown colour name
                 and a malformed hex are all QUOTED LITERALS, so all
                 three are caught at PARSE time with a line and column,
                 not discovered mid-frame. Runtime colour errors simply
                 do not exist.
             (i) `push` / `pop` of the pen state stays deferred until
                 something actually needs it.
       [x] 80.6 DECIDED (2026-09-21): the geometry verbs -- the
             smallest set that draws a real textbook figure. Every
             coordinate is user units mapped by 80.4; every LENGTH (r,
             w, h) is user units scaled by s; every one of them honours
             the pen state of 80.5 and fills when filling is on.

                 move   x, y                 pen position, no ink
                 rmove  dx, dy               ... relative
                 lineto x, y                 draw from the pen to here
                 rline  dx, dy               ... relative
                 line   x1, y1, x2, y2       a one-off segment
                 point  x, y                 a dot
                 circle x, y, r
                 arc    x, y, r, a0, a1
                 rect   x, y, w, h
                 poly   x1, y1, x2, y2, ...  closed polygon
                 path   x1, y1, x2, y2, ...  open polyline

             (a) ONLY move/rmove/lineto/rline/line TOUCH THE PEN
                 POSITION; the shapes are placed absolutely and leave
                 it alone. `line` leaves the pen at its second point so
                 a `line` then `rline` chain works. The pen starts each
                 frame at the user origin (0,0) -- predictable, and
                 where a physics figure starts anyway.
             (b) `lineto` earns its place by completing the pen model:
                 move/lineto/rmove/rline IS HPGL's PU/PD/PR set, which
                 80.1 chose. `line` stays the four-argument convenience
                 for a one-off segment.
             (c) ANGLES ARE DEGREES, following PostScript (`arc x y r
                 ang1 ang2`) and HPGL -- what someone drawing a plate
                 actually writes. The help must then say, loudly, that
                 `sin`/`cos` inside an EXPRESSION still take radians,
                 because that is genuinely a mixed convention and
                 pretending otherwise will not help anybody. Any later
                 Logo-style `turn` (80.1) is degrees too.
             (d) ARC SWEEP IS SIGNED: from a0 to a1, positive
                 counter-clockwise in user space, so `arc 0,0,10,90,0`
                 is a clockwise quarter. GOTCHA, and exactly the kind
                 that costs an hour: our y flip means a device angle is
                 the NEGATED user angle, so a counter-clockwise user
                 sweep is a clockwise device sweep -- the call is
                 cairo_arc_negative (xc, yc, r, -a0, -a1), not
                 cairo_arc.
             (e) CIRCLES AND THE `stretch` PROPERTY (80.4h) collide:
                 with aspect preserved a circle is a circle, but under
                 stretch it must become an ellipse, and cairo_arc
                 cannot draw one. Draw circles and arcs inside a
                 cairo_save / translate-to-centre / scale (|sx|/s,
                 |sy|/s) / restore -- a no-op in the normal
                 aspect-preserved case, and correct under stretch.
             (f) `rect x,y,w,h` takes the LOWER-LEFT corner, because
                 y points up and any other choice needs explaining.
                 Negative w or h extends the other way. No centred
                 variant: `rect cx-w/2, cy-h/2, w, h` already says it,
                 and every verb we do not add is one the help page does
                 not have to document.
             (g) `poly` CLOSES, `path` DOES NOT -- two verbs over one
                 implementation, which kills the "does it close?"
                 question before anyone asks it. `poly` fills when fill
                 is on; `path` only ever strokes, so there is no
                 implicit-closure-for-fill subtlety to explain. Both
                 want an even argument count and at least three points
                 (80.2 rule 8); `path` with two points is just `line`.
             (h) `point x,y` draws a filled disc of radius = the
                 current line width, in the STROKE colour -- so `width`
                 sizes the dots and no new state appears. It is what
                 marks a hinge, a mass or an intersection.
             (i) Non-finite or degenerate arguments skip the statement
                 rather than erroring, per 80.10 -- a radius passing
                 through zero as a knob winds is a value, not a bug.
       [x] 80.7 DECIDED (2026-09-21): text -- which carries more of the
             lesson than it looks, because a figure in this style is
             half annotation: A, B, theta, l1.

                 text  x, y, "format" [, expr, ...]
                 align "h" [, "v"]        -- pen state, see 80.5

             (a) ONE VERB, NOT TWO. The planned `value x, y, expr,
                 decimals` is gone: `text x, y, "%.2f", v` says the
                 same thing in the same space, and one verb is one help
                 paragraph and one implementation.
             (b) WHICH FORCED ALIGNMENT OUT OF THE ARGUMENT LIST -- the
                 clash that decided the shape. With variadic format
                 arguments a trailing `"centre"` is ambiguous: is it an
                 alignment or a format argument? Making alignment PEN
                 STATE removes the ambiguity and is the more HPGL-ish
                 answer anyway, since it belongs beside font size
                 rather than on every label. 80.5's state vector and
                 verb table are updated to match.
             (c) THE FORMAT STRING IS VALIDATED AT PARSE TIME, and this
                 one is safety, not style: handing a user-typed format
                 to printf with double arguments is how `%s`
                 dereferences a double and `%n` writes to the stack.
                 Accept only `%%` and the numeric conversions (%f %e %g
                 %F %E %G) with optional flags, width and precision;
                 require the conversion count to equal the number of
                 trailing expressions; make anything else a parse error
                 with its line and column. No format string reaches
                 printf unchecked, ever.
             (d) `\n` SPLITS LINES, drawn downward at 1.2x the font
                 size, each line anchored per the alignment -- so a
                 four-line bench card is one statement.
             (e) PANGO, not the cairo toy API. The project uses both
                 (clock, meter and dial go through pango_cairo; graph,
                 table and file viewer use cairo_show_text), and text
                 here IS the teaching content -- a plate wants theta,
                 lambda and Omega to render properly. Size via
                 pango_font_description_set_absolute_size (size_user *
                 s * PANGO_SCALE).
             (f) ALIGNMENT BREAKS 80.4(i)'s PURITY, and it is worth
                 admitting rather than discovering: the core half
                 cannot measure glyphs without a font backend, so core
                 emits the anchor point plus the alignment enum and the
                 painter does the extents and the offset. 80.12's
                 headless tests assert the anchor and the enum, not a
                 glyph box. This is the one place the core/painter seam
                 is not a clean "core computes, painter strokes".
             (g) DEFAULT ALIGNMENT IS "centre", "middle", because
                 `text x, y, "A"` almost always means "label this
                 point", not "start writing here". The conventional
                 left/baseline is one `align` away.
             (h) Glyphs come out upright for free, since 80.4(a) keeps
                 the flip out of the CTM. ROTATED labels -- along an
                 axis, along a ray -- are a real want and a real
                 omission: they need a CTM rotation about the anchor,
                 and they go on 80.19's list rather than into v1.
             (i) Font FAMILY stays a node property (80.5), so one
                 figure is typographically consistent and the family is
                 not repeated on every line.
       [ ] 80.8 Inputs become variables. Follow Calculator 2: an `inputs`
             count property with renameable ports, each input's message
             members bound as variables (the port's name, plus its named
             members), last value per input retained so the figure
             redraws whole when any one input updates. A fresh node with
             nothing wired must still draw something -- unbound variables
             evaluate to 0 rather than erroring, or the node is blank and
             mysterious until the first message.
       [ ] 80.9 Implementation shape, the now-standard split: PnFigure in
             libpipnode-core holds the program, the parse, the variable
             bindings and the error state; pn-figure-gui.c installs the
             cairo painter as PnNodeClass.paint_plot and is registered
             from pn_gui_install_builtin_nodes(). Client area sized like
             PnPlot's (fixed w/h constants to start); the zoom overlay,
             the drop shadow and the repaint plumbing then come for free.
             No ABI bump -- this adds a node, not a vfunc.
       [ ] 80.10 Errors the user can actually see. A parse failure names
             the line number and the offending statement, sets
             pn_node_set_has_error() so the node paints red, and shows
             the message in the client area instead of a half-drawn
             figure. Nothing to stdout or g_warning -- pipnode launches
             from a desktop launcher with no terminal. A runtime NaN or
             infinity in one statement skips that statement rather than
             killing the frame.
       [ ] 80.11 Editing the program: pn_param_spec_set_multiline() plus
             full width, or its own dialog tab the way Expression2 gives
             the expression a tab of its own. Twenty lines of drawing
             code does not fit a one-line entry.
       [ ] 80.12 Unit tests in tests/unit/, headless and pixel-free: feed
             a program plus variable bindings, dump the evaluated display
             list (verb + resolved numbers) and compare text. That covers
             the parser, the expression binding, the pen state machine
             and the view transform without rendering anything. Cover the
             error paths too -- bad verb, wrong argument count, unclosed
             string, division by zero.
       [ ] 80.13 Prove it runs headless: ./src/pipnode-run -t 3 on a
             worksheet containing a Figure must parse and evaluate the
             program with no GTK, no cairo and no CRITICALs. The painter
             lives in the gui tier; nothing in the core half may reach
             for it.
       [ ] 80.14 Help page data/help/PnFigure.html (core node -> data/,
             listed in data/Makefile.am): every verb with its arguments,
             the coordinate convention, the pen model, the message
             contract, and three worked figures short enough to paste.
             This page IS the language specification -- there is nowhere
             else it can live.
       [ ] 80.15 Example worksheets, the reason the node exists. At least
             two: a simple lever or balance beam whose arms tilt with an
             input, and a ray-optics bench where an object distance knob
             moves the object, the rays and the image together. Mind
             TODO #79.2 -- several examples/ subdirectories are still
             missing from examples/Makefile.am, so check the new one
             actually installs.
       [ ] 80.16 ANIMATION, and not before the above works. When an
             argument evaluates to a VECTOR the figure becomes a film:
             frame i takes element i of every vector argument, scalars
             broadcast unchanged, and the frame count is the SHORTEST
             vector -- corrected from "longest" by 80.3(f), because
             vector OP vector already truncates to the shorter operand
             and the figure must not disagree with its own arithmetic.
             Also from 80.3(f): a comparison collapses a vector to one
             scalar, so `blue = s > 0` cannot vary per frame; if
             per-frame conditionals are wanted they need arithmetic
             (mask idioms), not comparisons. This otherwise falls
             straight out of PnVarStore already dealing in
             scalar-or-vector values, and it means a Ramp into a
             Calculator into a Figure animates with no new plumbing --
             the same pipeline the Compute examples already build.
       [ ] 80.17 Animation controls: frames-per-second, and a play mode
             (once / loop / ping-pong / hold-last). The frame timer and
             frame index live in the core half so the behaviour is the
             node's, not the painter's; each tick calls
             pn_node_request_repaint(). Expose `frame` (the index) and
             `t` (0..1 through the film) as variables so a program can
             animate things that did not arrive as a vector. Stop the
             timer in dispose(), and do not run it at all while the
             figure has no vector argument.
       [ ] 80.18 A design tension to settle before 80.16 is coded, not
             after: a vector means FRAMES, so what draws twenty identical
             ticks along an axis? Candidate answer is a `repeat n` verb
             binding an index variable for the statements that follow --
             which reintroduces the blocks 80.2 refused. Decide then:
             either a one-statement repeat prefix, or an explicit
             `repeat` / `end` pair, or nothing at all and let people
             write the twenty lines out. Do not let it creep in by
             accident.
       [ ] 80.19 Later primitives, in the order a physics plate wants
             them: arrows (a figure of forces without arrowheads is not a
             figure of forces), ROTATED LABELS along an axis or a ray
             (deferred out of 80.7(h), and the first of these anyone
             will miss), angle-mark arcs with a label, dimension lines
             with ticks, hatching for a wall or a ground plane, bezier
             curves, and an `axes` helper that draws a labelled pair of
             axes in one statement. Each one is a verb plus a
             paragraph in the help page; none of them changes the engine.
       [ ] 80.20 Export, if it ever earns its place: the display list is
             backend-agnostic, so the same walk that drives cairo could
             emit SVG for a figure someone wants in a document, or a PNG
             sequence for an animated one. Note it and move on -- v1
             ships without it.
       [ ] 80.21 GUI check by hand, at the end: wind the input knobs and
             watch the figure track them; drag a Figure into the zoom
             overlay and confirm it scales cleanly (vector drawing, so it
             must); break the program mid-edit and confirm the node turns
             red with a readable line number instead of drawing garbage;
             then wire a vector in and watch it move.

[ ] 81 Multi-argument functions and constants in the calculator
       language. Today PnVarStore knows exactly ten functions, all of
       them unary -- sin, cos, tan, log, log10, exp, sqrt, abs, floor,
       ceil -- and not one constant, which is why TODO #79.5 writes
       3.14159265 out by hand. Every Calculator and Calculator 2 in
       every worksheet shares that gap, and the Figure node (#80) walks
       straight into it: a mechanics figure wants atan2(dy,dx) in its
       first ten lines. Raised by 80.3(d) and 80.3(e), which also
       settled that the figure node must NOT grow a function table of
       its own -- the fix belongs here, in shared core, once.

       [ ] 81.1 Settle the list, deliberately, rather than importing
             libm. Constants: `pi`, `e`. Two-argument functions:
             `atan2(y,x)` (the one that forced this), `min`, `max`,
             `pow` and `hypot`. One-argument additions that are
             conspicuous by their absence next to sin/cos/tan: `asin`,
             `acos`, `atan`, `round`, `trunc`, `sign`. Hyperbolics and
             the rest stay out until something asks for them.
       [ ] 81.2 Represent the extra arguments WITHOUT growing
             PnExprNode. For a CALL the struct's `right` field is
             unused today (checked) and pn_expr_node_free() already
             recurses into it, so argument two chains off `right` the
             way a statement sequence does, and sizeof (PnExprNode)
             never changes. That matters more than it looks:
             pn-expr-parser.h is INSTALLED public API (it reaches
             plugins through pipnode.h and pipnodeinclude_HEADERS), so
             growing the struct is an ABI hazard of the same class as
             appending a PnNodeClass vfunc -- it would mean bumping
             PN_PLUGIN_ABI_VERSION and reinstalling every plugin, with
             the usual stale-plugin dlopen deadlock for anyone who
             forgets. Chaining avoids the bump entirely. Existing
             single-argument trees stay bit-for-bit what they are
             (right == NULL).
       [ ] 81.3 Parser: parse_factor_body() currently takes exactly one
             expression between the parens. Accept a comma-separated
             list, and reject the wrong count at PARSE time with the
             position the lexer already tracks -- "atan2 takes 2
             arguments, got 1 at position 14" -- rather than at
             evaluation, so a bad expression lights the node up the
             moment it is typed.
       [ ] 81.4 Where the arity table lives, since both halves need it:
             the parser needs the arity to validate, the evaluator
             needs the implementation, and the parser must not start
             depending on the var store. Put both in a new
             pn-expr-funcs.[ch] -- name, arity, and a function pointer
             -- and have pn-var-store.c dispatch through it instead of
             its private builtin_fns[]. One table, one place to add the
             eleventh function.
       [ ] 81.5 Vector semantics must MATCH the binary operators
             exactly, or the language grows two rules for one idea:
             scalar broadcasts, vector-with-vector is elementwise and
             TRUNCATES TO THE SHORTER operand, per 80.3(f). That
             applies to every two-argument function -- min, max, atan2,
             pow, hypot alike.
       [ ] 81.6 Constants resolve as a FALLBACK in the VARIABLE case,
             after the bindings, not by pre-binding them into the
             store: pn_var_store_clear() drops every binding, so a
             pre-bound `pi` would evaporate on the next clear and come
             back only if every caller remembered to re-add it. As a
             fallback it cannot be lost, and a data-bag member actually
             named `pi` still shadows it, which is the conservative way
             round. Once this lands, delete the Figure node's own
             pi/e pre-bind from 80.3(c) -- it exists only because this
             entry did not.
       [ ] 81.7 No new operators, and say so out loud: `^` stays XOR
             and there is no `**`. Squaring is still a multiplication
             or pow(x,2). Every existing worksheet must mean exactly
             what it meant before -- this entry only ADDS names.
       [ ] 81.8 Tests, extending the two that already exist rather than
             starting new binaries: test-pn-expr-parser.c gets the
             arity errors, nested calls (atan2(atan2(1,2),3)), a
             trailing comma, an empty argument; test-pn-var-store.c
             gets the values, the vector broadcast and truncation for
             two-argument functions, the constants, and a binding named
             `pi` shadowing the constant. Usual conventions -- pntest.h
             harness, headless, ./run-unit-tests.sh green.
       [ ] 81.9 Help. The function list is written out in TWO node help
             pages -- data/help/PnExpression.html and
             PnExpression2.html, both saying "Functions (one
             argument)" -- so both need splitting into one- and
             two-argument lists plus a Constants line. The doc comments
             in pn-expr-parser.h (the grammar paragraph on
             pn_expr_parser_parse) and pn-var-store.h say the same
             thing a third and fourth time; update them too, and add
             the new names to #80's PnFigure.html when it is written.
       [ ] 81.10 Sweep for the workarounds this removes: TODO #79.5's
             literal 3.14159265, and any example worksheet doing the
             same by hand. Then verify the whole point of 81.2 -- an
             existing worksheet with a Calculator in it loads and
             computes unchanged, and NO plugin ABI bump was needed. Say
             that in the commit message, so nobody goes looking for a
             reinstall that is not required.
